      "notes": "All nine zero limits now refuse inspect/read/write before callbacks, provider reads or staging. Prior actual short-read, Windows locked-byte write, exact-name collision, cancellation and target/source preservation proofs remain. Disk-full/flush/close faults and other platforms remain broader obligations; a write lock does not qualify them. Legacy TIFF encoding/publication now preserves a pre-existing fixed-suffix file, checked by a real failing regression before repair. Memory encoding removes the predictable sibling and disk readback; shared byte publication uses the existing exclusive atomic helper. This does not qualify disk-full/flush/close or POSIX ownership faults. The shared panel now revalidates held input identity after a read worker is ready and before publication. An actual changed-file regression fails against the prior installed product and passes after repair. This does not qualify remaining disk-full/flush/close or POSIX faults. A reproduced Windows/Linux callback-CWD destination-redirection bug is repaired in immediate/deferred/streaming helpers. Session input/output freeze paths before user hooks, and empty/NUL paths refuse before callbacks. Eight new Python path cases plus the existing native session atomicity case pass in source/installed contexts; native14 includes the new helper and existing session IO suite. Installed core-only C++ SDK verifies session paths without TIFF/GUI/Python imports. Test-only Linux production-helper builds now qualify actual ENOSPC in an isolated exactly1MiB tmpfs (independent raw control errno28 after1044480bytes), target/source/directory preservation through all three writers, and process-scoped file-size-limit cases. These are helper-level Linux checks, not Linux TIFF/package or Windows disk-full acceptance. Flush/close failure qualification, POSIX pathname substitution ownership and remaining instrumentation remain open.",
